  by L1 (e): both model 7.56925; a1 from the reference -> 36.94743; p_off from the reference -> 33.69750; both -> 0.00369
  The 'ref/ref' row is a closure check, not a result: it must come back at the reference. If it does not, the chain is broken and no attribution holds.
  WHAT THE SINGLE SWAPS ARE, and what they cannot say. Each row's deviation from ref/ref is exactly one error array: ref/model isolates the p_off error, model/ref the a1 error, model/model both. But
  a1*E + prior = plane_mean(a3*E) holds by construction on BOTH sides, so the two coefficients are complementary halves of one decomposition and a reference half paired with a model half breaks a cancellation
  the reference itself relies on -- measured on the reference side as a1*E rms against plane_mean(P_z) rms below. So a single swap being worse does NOT indict that coefficient, and 'whichever swap helps most'
  is not an available reading. What the rows DO give is the size of each error's uncompensated effect and how much the two errors cancel when both are present.
  Plane-averaged 1-D only; no lateral 3-D error is in any of it.
  uncompensated: p_off error alone 36.94743 e, a1 error alone 33.69750 e, both together 7.56925 e -> the two coefficient errors cancel 4.7-fold in L1.
  IS THE DIAGNOSIS A REPAIR LIST? No, and this is the exact number rather than a bound. Dividing prior by its own best gain 1.551 -- the single change that removes 85.9% of its rms error -- gives charge L1 34.52551 e against the baseline's 7.56925 e,
  a factor of 4.56. Fixing the covariance gain ALONE makes the bound charge WORSE, because it removes a compensation the wrong a1 currently relies on. The two halves have to move together.
  The two halves are NOT equally tractable, though, and the binding constraint is a1: prior's error is 85.9% removable by one scalar, so a joint change has a cheap well-specified handle on that half, while
  a1's is 7.2% removable by any scalar and 77.7% pure cavity, so its half has no scalar handle at all and needs the cavity or the grid it is computed on.
  That cancellation is partly structural, not an independent finding: the model's own closure satisfies the same identity with its own fields, and the model's bound-charge total is close to the reference,
  so the two errors are largely forced to oppose. It is reported as a magnitude, not read as a correlation between independent errors.

